#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ezyspeech: install, update and run EzySpeech, natively or in Docker.

    ezyspeech status [--json]          what is installed, running, and available
    ezyspeech version                  the installed version
    ezyspeech check-update [--json]    is there a newer release?
    ezyspeech update [--yes] [--to V]  install the latest (or V), roll back if it fails
    ezyspeech rollback                 go back to the previous release
    ezyspeech start | stop | restart
    ezyspeech logs [-f] [-n N]
    ezyspeech password [--generate] [--json]
    ezyspeech backup [FILE]            settings, secrets and transcripts, as .tar.gz
    ezyspeech uninstall [--purge]      --purge also deletes settings and transcripts
    ezyspeech menu                     the same, as screens
    ezyspeech install --mode native|docker ...   (normally run by install.sh)

Both kinds of install keep each release beside the last one:

    <home>/releases/<version>/   the code (and, natively, its own Python venv)
    <home>/current               a link to the one in use

An update downloads the release from GitHub, checks it against the
published SHA-256 sums, prepares it next to the running one, switches the
link and restarts; if the new one does not come up healthy within a minute
it switches back. A native install keeps its settings and transcripts in
/var/lib/ezyspeech, a Docker one in the volume ezyspeech_ezyspeech; neither
is touched by an update.

The install's mode and paths are in /etc/ezyspeech/install.conf (or
~/.config/ezyspeech/install.conf for a Docker install made without root).

Python 3.8+ standard library only: this runs before anything is installed.

